Taking this one.

Confirmed both ends in the source:

- `packages/core/src/file-mutation.ts` raises `StaleContentError` from `writeIfUnchanged`, purely on a byte comparison under the per-path lock:

  ```ts
  const current = yield* fs.readFile(input.target.canonical)
  if (!sameBytes(current, input.expected)) {
    return yield* new StaleContentError({ path: input.target.canonical })
  }
  ```

- `packages/core/src/tool/edit.ts` is the only place that maps it, and it's the mapping that introduces the permission wording — the error itself carries just a `path`.

So the message names a cause the error has no knowledge of. Any writer touching the file inside the read→write window produces it, permission flow or not.

I'll switch it to the wording you suggested, `File changed since it was read. Read it again before editing.`, which describes the actual condition and keeps the same remedy. `packages/core/test/tool-edit.test.ts` pins the current string, so that assertion gets updated in the same PR — which also keeps the change provably scoped to this one message.

